{"name":"Etched Glass","hex":"#dde2e2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"etched","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Cut or dug into the surface as by etching.","Toned and with a well-defined musculature."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of etch"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/etched"},{"term":"glass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An amorphous solid, often transparent substance, usually made by melting silica sand with various additives (for most purposes, a mixture of soda, potash and lime is added).","Any amorphous solid (one without a regular crystal lattice)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fit with glass; to glaze.","To enclose in glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glass etching","blocks":[{"text":"Glass etching, or \"French embossing\", is a popular technique developed during the mid-1800s that is still widely used in both residential and commercial spaces today. Glass etching comprises the techniques of creating art on the surface of glass by applying acidic, caustic, or abrasive substances. Traditionally this is done after the glass is blown or cast, although mold-etching has replaced some forms of surface etching. The removal of minute amounts of glass causes the characteristic rough surface and translucent quality of frosted glass."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Bankfield_Museum_053.jpg/330px-Bankfield_Museum_053.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass_etching"},{"title":"Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Glass is an amorphous (non-crystalline) solid. Because it is often transparent and chemically inert, glass has found widespread practical, technological, and decorative use in window panes, tableware, and optics. Some common objects made of glass are named after the material, e.g., a \"glass\" for drinking, \"glasses\" for vision correction, and a \"magnifying glass\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg/330px-Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Pride-window.jpg/500px-Pride-window.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Pride-window.jpg/500px-Pride-window.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Etched glass and stained glass commemorative window (c. 2006)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APride-window.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Chinese_-_Bowl_with_Design_of_Flowering_Peony_-_Walters_47679.jpg/120px-Chinese_-_Bowl_with_Design_of_Flowering_Peony_-_Walters_47679.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Chinese_-_Bowl_with_Design_of_Flowering_Peony_-_Walters_47679.jpg/250px-Chinese_-_Bowl_with_Design_of_Flowering_Peony_-_Walters_47679.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Chinese_-_Bowl_with_Design_of_Flowering_Peony_-_Walters_47679.jpg/120px-Chinese_-_Bowl_with_Design_of_Flowering_Peony_-_Walters_47679.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChinese_-_Bowl_with_Design_of_Flowering_Peony_-_Walters_47679.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/30._INTERIOR%2C_MODEL_HALL%2C_DOOR.jpg/120px-30._INTERIOR%2C_MODEL_HALL%2C_DOOR.jpg?utm_so